window.MD102_V7_PATHS=[{"id":"identity","title":"Device identity is not device management","domain":"Infrastructure","lab":"I01","console":"enrollment","objectiveIds":["INF-001","INF-002","INF-003"],"difficulty":"Foundation","time":"30–45 min","goal":"Explain what Microsoft Entra registration, Entra join, and Intune enrollment each establish, and choose an appropriate path for a personal or company-owned device.","chapters":[{"title":"Begin with three different questions","teach":"A device identity answers whether a device is known to Microsoft Entra. Enrollment answers whether a management service such as Intune can deliver policies and collect management signals. Compliance answers whether an enrolled device meets stated requirements. These are related but independent states. A device can be registered in Entra but not enrolled in Intune; being registered does not mean administrators can configure BitLocker or deploy Win32 apps. A device can be enrolled yet report noncompliant when it has not met required settings. Whenever a colleague says 'the device is in Azure', ask precisely which of these three states they mean.","example":"A contractor signs in to a company app on a personal iPhone. The organization recognizes the device for sign-in, but may manage only the corporate app data rather than the whole phone.","visual":["Entra identity","Intune enrollment","Policies + check-in","Compliance evaluation","Conditional Access decision"],"error":"Mistaking 'Entra registered' for 'fully managed' and assuming a policy must arrive on the phone."},{"title":"Registered, joined, hybrid joined","teach":"Entra registered generally suits personal devices that retain personal-device ownership. Entra joined suits corporate Windows devices that use cloud identity for workplace sign-in. Hybrid Entra joined also connects a device to an on-premises Active Directory domain and introduces synchronization and network dependencies. These are not different names for the same connection. Choose based on the device's ownership, identity architecture, and sign-in experience—not solely on the desire to run a security policy. Hybrid join may be required by legacy workloads, but introduces infrastructure that should not be adopted without need.","example":"A new corporate laptop intended for a cloud-first organization usually has a different enrollment plan than an existing domain-joined workstation with legacy Kerberos dependencies.","visual":["Personal + registered","Corporate + Entra joined","Legacy domain + hybrid","Management as separate enrollment"],"error":"Selecting hybrid join for a cloud-only fleet despite having no domain controllers."},{"title":"Enrollment is a deliberate second step","teach":"Windows automatic MDM enrollment depends on tenant MDM settings, eligible identities and licensing, supported device versions, user/device scope, and reachable Microsoft endpoints. A successful Entra join by itself is not proof of Intune enrollment. The Device Management Enrollment Service, enrollment restrictions, ownership, platform, and MDM scope all matter. On a real tenant, compare the device entry in Entra with the managed device entry in Intune and verify the management authority, enrollment date, last check-in and reported policy status.","example":"If the Entra device exists but Intune All devices has no matching managed entry, investigate MDM scope, enrollment restrictions, license assignment, and enrollment errors before recreating the device.","visual":["Join or register","User in MDM scope?","License + restrictions","Enroll and report","First policy sync"],"error":"Repeatedly pressing Sync on a device that was never enrolled."},{"title":"Prove the final state with evidence","teach":"A realistic administrator collects different evidence for different claims: Entra device object for identity, Intune managed device properties for enrollment, per-policy status for configuration, and compliance details for requirements. Microsoft Entra Conditional Access may use compliance as a grant control only after the relevant device and sign-in signals exist. When troubleshooting, identify the failed transition rather than rerunning all prior steps. This is also safer than wiping the user's device as a first reaction.","example":"Ticket: 'Laptop can sign in to Office, but it cannot receive company settings.' Confirm the join state, Intune enrollment authority, license and MDM scope, then policy assignment and last sync in that order.","visual":["Identity evidence","Enrollment evidence","Policy evidence","Compliance evidence","Access sign-in logs"],"error":"Treating successful Office sign-in as evidence of policy deployment."}],"questions":[{"q":"A personal iPhone can sign in to Outlook but is absent from Intune managed devices. What is the best initial conclusion?","a":["The phone may have an Entra identity or app-level controls without full-device MDM enrollment.","The phone must be fully Intune-managed because Outlook works.","All Apple enrollments automatically create the same management record.","Conditional Access can never apply to that session."],"why":"Application sign-in and device management are different processes. Determine whether the organization uses app protection or actual MDM enrollment before troubleshooting."},{"q":"A new Windows laptop is Entra joined but never receives an Intune compliance policy. Which evidence should you check first?","a":["Whether the device is actually enrolled in Intune and is in the correct MDM enrollment scope.","Whether the user likes the company wallpaper.","Whether a BitLocker key has already been rotated three times.","Whether the laptop appears in the Microsoft Store."],"why":"Join does not guarantee MDM enrollment; confirm the management channel exists before checking policy application."},{"q":"Which source best proves that an assigned Intune policy reached a managed device?","a":["The device's per-policy status together with recent management check-in information.","The device name appearing in Entra ID.","A user receiving an email with a policy title.","The user's Windows desktop opening successfully."],"why":"Identity and sign-in are not enough; effective deployment requires device-side reporting and a current check-in."}],"artifact":null,"docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/devices/overview"},{"id":"groups","title":"Target the right users and devices","domain":"Infrastructure","lab":"I02","console":"groups","objectiveIds":["INF-004","INF-011","INF-012"],"difficulty":"Foundation","time":"35–45 min","goal":"Differentiate group membership, policy assignments, filters, role-based permissions and scope tags; create an appropriate pilot group.","chapters":[{"title":"Membership is the audience, not the permission","teach":"Microsoft Entra groups can organize users and devices for targeting. Assigned membership is chosen explicitly; dynamic membership uses a rule that evaluates device or user properties. Intune uses groups to deliver eligible configuration, app and compliance policy assignments. A group appearing in the tenant does not mean an administrator has rights to edit everything belonging to it. A scope tag limits visibility for eligible Intune objects, while RBAC permissions and scope groups govern permitted actions and the managed population. Keeping those concepts separate prevents accidental broad deployments and unsafe privilege assignments.","example":"Northstar creates a small Windows pilot group to try a new USB restriction before targeting 200 devices. The help desk receives a narrowly scoped role, not Global Administrator.","visual":["Group membership","Policy assignment","Filter evaluation","Intune permissions","Device check-in"],"error":"Mistaking a scope tag for a deployment target and wondering why no devices receive the setting."},{"title":"Assigned versus dynamic groups","teach":"Use assigned groups when the exact membership should be reviewed and controlled manually, especially for a small pilot. Use dynamic groups when membership should adapt to declared properties such as operating system or department. Dynamic rules need carefully defined properties and must be tested with representative objects; a user-based rule is not necessarily appropriate for shared devices. Changes to membership can take time, and the population may change unexpectedly if source properties are incorrect. Monitor both membership and assignment results before declaring a broad deployment successful.","example":"A pilot named 'Learning - Windows Pilot' includes corporate Windows devices. A new device joining the eligible population can become a member without a manual list edit.","visual":["Rule definition","Membership processing","Resulting cohort","Policy inclusion","Policy exclusion"],"error":"A dynamic device group matches a personal Windows laptop because the ownership predicate was forgotten."},{"title":"RBAC and scope tags answer different questions","teach":"Role-based access control asks what an administrator is authorized to do. Scope groups limit which users or devices a role assignment can manage. Scope tags help scope the visibility of objects in supported Intune administrative experiences. None of these replaces the policy assignment itself. Use least privilege: an operator who only needs to troubleshoot devices should not gain broad tenant administration rights. Test restricted access with a separate low-privilege identity and document which objects are visible and which changes are denied.","example":"A regional help desk should see only supported regional devices and perform permitted actions. Its ability to view a configuration profile does not automatically authorize editing every other profile.","visual":["Role permissions","Scope groups","Scope tags","Resulting console view"],"error":"Assigning the highest directory role when only limited help desk functions are required."},{"title":"Practice safe rollout and validate membership","teach":"Create the smallest representative target group, inspect actual members, assign a single test policy, synchronize an eligible pilot device, and inspect the assignment result. Verify that non-target devices did not receive the policy. For production rollouts, document change owner, exposure, success criteria, monitoring and rollback. If an assignment fails, check scope, group processing, enrollment and platform applicability instead of widening the audience to All devices.","example":"The pilot contains 14 eligible laptops; the test should report whether those laptops are assigned and whether a nonpilot device remains unaffected.","visual":["Design scope","Inspect members","Assign policy","Verify affected devices","Expand carefully"],"error":"Troubleshooting by assigning All devices and unintentionally impacting staff outside the pilot."}],"questions":[{"q":"A policy has a scope tag but no device group assignment. What should an administrator expect?","a":["The scope tag alone will not deploy the policy to devices.","The scope tag automatically targets every device.","Only Android devices will receive it.","A scope tag starts a full tenant sync."],"why":"Scope tags govern administrative visibility; device/user assignment controls deployment targeting."},{"q":"What is an advantage of a dynamic group for a large device fleet?","a":["Membership can adapt when supported device properties change.","It automatically overrides all Intune RBAC rules.","It guarantees every policy succeeds on every platform.","It removes the need to check group evaluation."],"why":"Dynamic rules compute membership using attributes; they do not override RBAC, applicability or policy state."},{"q":"A help desk member must troubleshoot only a regional pilot. What is the safest design?","a":["A narrowly permitted Intune role, appropriate scope groups and relevant scope tags.","Global Administrator for the entire tenant.","Only a broad device configuration policy.","A renamed group without any role assignment."],"why":"Least privilege uses actual action permissions and resource scope, tested using a limited account."}],"artifact":{"collection":"groups","name":"Learning - Windows Pilot","checks":{"rule":"Windows"},"proof":"A newly created group named Learning - Windows Pilot with the Windows membership rule, plus eligible matching devices."},"docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/mem/intune-service/fundamentals/role-based-access-control"},{"id":"appleandroid","title":"Choose Apple and Android enrollment correctly","domain":"Infrastructure","lab":"I04","console":"enrollment","objectiveIds":["INF-007","INF-008","INF-009","INF-010"],"difficulty":"Intermediate","time":"35–50 min","goal":"Select ownership-appropriate mobile management, identify Apple and Android integration requirements, and respect personal-device privacy.","chapters":[{"title":"Ownership changes the management boundary","teach":"A personally owned device and a company-owned device create different expectations around privacy, available controls, and deployment. For BYOD, app protection or user-centric enrollment can restrict business data without necessarily giving IT full device control. Corporate devices can use supervision or fully managed modes to enable broader controls. Choose device enrollment only when the business requires device-level management, not automatically because an employee needs Outlook. Always communicate what IT can see, restrict or remove.","example":"A nurse uses a personal phone for Teams. Protecting Teams and Outlook data through an app protection policy may be appropriate where supported, without issuing a full device wipe capability.","visual":["Ownership","App-only vs device MDM","Supported control","Consent/privacy","Verify result"],"error":"Using full wipe as the routine response to a lost personal phone with only corporate app data at risk."},{"title":"Apple deployment depends on trust services","teach":"Apple MDM integration uses the Apple Push Notification service and appropriate enrollment mechanisms. Corporate enrollment may use Apple Business Manager and Automated Device Enrollment, with supervision providing additional controls on eligible devices. The correct setup depends on account connections, device assignment to an MDM server, enrollment profiles, ownership, and supported OS versions. A device purchased outside the corporate channel may require a different path. Certificates and tokens have lifecycles; renewing them incorrectly can disrupt management.","example":"A business-owned iPad is assigned in Apple Business Manager but not enrolling. Inspect the MDM server assignment, Intune token status, enrollment profile and device activation path.","visual":["ABM device assignment","APNs / token health","Enrollment profile","Activation and supervision","Intune management"],"error":"Assuming Apple Business Manager assignment itself creates an Intune managed-device record immediately."},{"title":"Android Enterprise has distinct enrollment modes","teach":"Android Enterprise offers work profiles for personal devices, fully managed corporate devices, corporate-owned devices with work profiles, and dedicated devices such as kiosks. These modes differ in management scope, app deployment and privacy. Managed Google Play is often part of the app workflow. For bulk corporate provisioning, QR, token, NFC where applicable, Android zero-touch or Samsung Knox Mobile Enrollment may participate. Supported capabilities depend on device, Android version, enrollment method and vendor. Choose the mode from the user experience and ownership constraints.","example":"Warehouse scanning devices shared by rotating shift workers usually need a different enrollment design than a personal Android phone that runs one corporate app.","visual":["Personal work profile","Corporate fully managed","Corporate work profile","Dedicated kiosk","App store integration"],"error":"Choosing a personally owned work-profile mode for a locked-down kiosk that must work without a dedicated personal user."},{"title":"Troubleshoot the actual enrollment transition","teach":"Start from the symptom: is the device blocked before registration, during enrollment token retrieval, after user sign-in, or only when the first policy arrives? Verify licenses, supported ownership, enrollment restrictions, Apple/Google connector health, network, and device eligibility. Check enrollment reports rather than repeatedly wiping devices. Enrollment problems can be service-side, identity-side, platform-side, or profile-side, and each requires a different remedy.","example":"An Android enrollment QR code fails after a device reset. Confirm the code/token validity, device network, Android Enterprise connection and selected enrollment method before trying a different management mode.","visual":["Identify failed stage","Inspect prerequisites","Check service/token","Retry narrowly","Confirm MDM record"],"error":"Changing five settings at once so you cannot tell which one fixed the problem."}],"questions":[{"q":"A personal Android phone needs company Outlook data protected without managing all personal apps. Which strategy is most appropriate?","a":["App protection where supported, or a personal work-profile model if device-level work separation is needed.","Corporate dedicated-device enrollment with kiosk restrictions.","Mandatory factory reset and full corporate ownership.","Disabling all data transfer restrictions."],"why":"BYOD requires selecting the minimum management scope that meets organizational protection needs."},{"q":"A company iPad is listed in Apple Business Manager but not enrolled in Intune. What is the most relevant next check?","a":["MDM server assignment, token/APNs health and applicable enrollment profile.","Whether the iPad wallpaper is corporate blue.","Windows Autopilot device hash.","Microsoft Defender security intelligence version on a Windows PC."],"why":"ABM registration and enrollment are separate stages requiring correctly configured integration and activation."},{"q":"A shared, single-purpose Android barcode scanner is to run only approved workplace apps. Which Android Enterprise mode is likely appropriate?","a":["Dedicated corporate-managed device, subject to vendor compatibility.","Personal BYOD work profile used by a named employee.","Entra registered Windows PC mode.","Apple User Enrollment."],"why":"Dedicated mode is designed for corporate-owned task-oriented devices."}],"artifact":null,"docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/mem/intune-service/enrollment/device-enrollment"},{"id":"compliance","title":"Configuration, compliance and Conditional Access","domain":"Infrastructure","lab":"I06","console":"compliance","objectiveIds":["INF-013","INF-014","SEC-047"],"difficulty":"Intermediate","time":"45–60 min","goal":"Explain and configure the difference between enforcing a setting, measuring compliance, and deciding whether an identity receives access.","chapters":[{"title":"Three policy engines, three jobs","teach":"A configuration or endpoint security policy tells the device which settings to apply. A compliance policy evaluates whether the device meets requirements such as encryption or supported operating system version. Microsoft Entra Conditional Access evaluates the sign-in context and can require a compliant device as a grant control. A policy that measures encryption does not automatically turn encryption on. Similarly, a device marked compliant does not itself create a Conditional Access policy. Memorizing these distinctions avoids one of the most expensive endpoint mistakes: confusing assessment with remediation.","example":"A laptop has BitLocker off. A compliance policy checks encryption and reports noncompliance; a separate encryption configuration is needed to enable encryption where supported.","visual":["Security policy: configure","Device: apply + report","Compliance: evaluate","Entra CA: access decision","Reports + sign-in logs"],"error":"Turning on a compliance rule and expecting it to encrypt disks without an enabling policy."},{"title":"Enrollment and group assignment must exist first","teach":"A managed device needs a supported operating system, enrollment method, eligibility for the chosen setting, and a target group or filter. A compliance policy that is not assigned to the device may not evaluate it as expected. At real sign-in time, Conditional Access depends on the identity/session context, the device identity signal, and applicable policy targeting. Configure appropriate exclusions for emergency access accounts and evaluate in report-only mode before enforcing new access controls. Don't use a blanket access block to debug an enrollment issue.","example":"A newly issued finance laptop is in the accounting group but not yet managed. Review the MDM enrollment, group membership and sync, then the compliance record before investigating the CA sign-in log.","visual":["Enroll","Target group","Apply configuration","Evaluate compliance","Review CA result"],"error":"Blocking all employees with an untested CA policy and losing recovery access."},{"title":"Read reporting outcomes accurately","teach":"An Intune policy can be created successfully yet have zero eligible targets. A device can show assignment pending until the next check-in. A setting may be not applicable because the platform is unsupported, not because the administrator has permission problems. A security setting can fail independently of overall compliance. When investigating, capture the device ID, OS version, latest check-in, assignment group membership, per-setting status and any compliance grace-period behavior. A green summary dashboard is not enough evidence of a correct deployment.","example":"You created Windows compliance policy 'Learning - Encryption Check'. It is assigned to Corporate Windows. Sync an enrolled matching device and inspect the per-policy result: Compliant or Noncompliant with a reason.","visual":["Created","Assigned","Pending","Evaluated","Compliant / noncompliant"],"error":"Assuming an assignment is successful when the device has not checked in for two weeks."},{"title":"Repair the missing control, then retest access","teach":"When encryption is missing, first determine whether the endpoint supports BitLocker and which encryption settings are intended. Pilot the necessary disk encryption configuration, ensure recovery-key management is safe, then allow device sync and status reporting. Next confirm compliance becomes compliant based on effective posture. Only then test the Conditional Access requirement with a pilot identity and review Entra sign-in logs. Distinguish policy configuration success, device compliance success and user access success in the change record.","example":"Evidence bundle: encryption policy status Succeeded, compliance record Compliant, CA grant requirement satisfied for a test user, and an unaffected emergency account exclusion.","visual":["Enable encryption","Verify key escrow","Sync + report","Check compliance","Test sign-in"],"error":"Disabling compliance enforcement to make access work without fixing the security requirement."}],"questions":[{"q":"An encryption compliance policy marks devices noncompliant. What will that compliance policy do by itself?","a":["Evaluate encryption posture, not enable BitLocker on the device.","Encrypt every enrolled disk automatically.","Rotate every local administrator password.","Deploy Microsoft 365 Apps."],"why":"Compliance evaluates a property; encryption settings are delivered by an appropriate configuration/security policy."},{"q":"What is the safest initial state for a new Conditional Access policy that may impact many employees?","a":["Pilot/report-only with emergency-access protections and sign-in impact review.","Immediately On for all users without exclusions.","Delete existing device registration before testing.","Ignore report-only results and trust the policy name."],"why":"Report-only and controlled scoping reduce lockout risk."},{"q":"A device is compliant in Intune, but a user cannot open a protected app. Which evidence is most relevant next?","a":["The user's Microsoft Entra sign-in logs and Conditional Access evaluation.","Only the computer's wallpaper.","Only the Windows update ring title.","Whether an unrelated Android tablet has an app installed."],"why":"Access can be blocked by policy, session or identity conditions beyond device compliance."}],"artifact":{"collection":"compliance","name":"Learning - Encryption Check","checks":{"platform":"Windows","requiresEncryption":true,"group":"Northstar - Corporate Windows"},"sync":true,"proof":"A new Windows encryption compliance policy targeted to Northstar - Corporate Windows, evaluated by syncing an enrolled matching device."},"docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/mem/intune-service/protect/device-compliance-get-started"},{"id":"autopilot","title":"Autopilot from new device to first sign-in","domain":"Management","lab":"M01","console":"autopilot","objectiveIds":["MAN-019","MAN-020","MAN-021","MAN-022","MAN-023"],"difficulty":"Intermediate","time":"40–55 min","goal":"Choose deployment and device preparation methods, explain Autopilot prerequisites, and design an Enrollment Status Page validation sequence.","chapters":[{"title":"Autopilot is a provisioning workflow, not an image copy","teach":"Windows Autopilot uses Microsoft cloud services and a device's registered identity to guide the out-of-box setup and subsequent enrollment. Its aim is to configure a Windows device without building and maintaining a traditional custom OS image for each machine. Autopilot depends on prerequisites including supported Windows edition, network access, licensing, registration/identity, deployment policy targeting and enrollment readiness. Deployment profiles, device preparation policies, and the Enrollment Status Page are separate concepts; choosing one does not automatically satisfy the others.","example":"A remote worker opens a freshly shipped laptop. The device reaches the company-branded setup, signs in, joins the tenant and receives management settings according to its eligible deployment path.","visual":["Device registration","Deployment policy","Out-of-box setup","Enroll into Intune","Apps + settings check"],"error":"Expecting an unregistered device to discover the correct Autopilot profile by its serial number alone."},{"title":"Different modes solve different business problems","teach":"User-driven deployment is commonly suited to a device assigned to an individual who signs in. Self-deploying can support shared, kiosk or other no-primary-user designs, but has specific hardware and attestation prerequisites. Pre-provisioning divides preparation into technician and user phases where supported. Windows Autopilot device preparation has its own prerequisites and setup model. Compare prerequisites and supported device identity, enrollment, network, and application behavior before choosing. Avoid assuming every mode has the same user interaction, profile structure or supported scenario.","example":"An accountant receives an individually assigned laptop, while a shared reception kiosk should not rely on the same primary-user setup and application experience.","visual":["User-driven","Pre-provisioning","Self-deploying","Device preparation","Pick by use case"],"error":"Choosing self-deploying for a device without meeting supported attestation prerequisites."},{"title":"Enrollment Status Page communicates readiness","teach":"The Enrollment Status Page (ESP) tracks setup and can block access until selected required device or user assignments are processed, subject to supported configuration. It is not a guarantee that every arbitrary application will install. Overly broad required-app choices or broken detection rules can trap device setup. Pilot with a small required-app set, check assignment intent and detection rules, and examine enrollment diagnostics if a device stops during setup. The same application that deploys later may behave differently in the early enrollment context.","example":"A pilot laptop remains on 'Installing apps'. Inspect the required Win32 app's detection rule, applicability and service check-in instead of replacing the laptop immediately.","visual":["Select required apps","ESP waits","Installation reports","Detection confirms","First usable desktop"],"error":"Treating successful creation of an ESP profile as proof that every required app installed."},{"title":"Plan pilot and verification before expansion","teach":"Create a narrowly targeted Autopilot profile, choose a supported mode, verify device identity or preparation-group membership, apply a naming convention, and confirm the enrollment profile is assigned. Validate the out-of-box experience on an authorized test device. Record device registration, join/enrollment, required app status, and user sign-in success. A local simulator can model the profile and assignment but cannot perform TPM attestation or a true cloud deployment. Never claim an actual Autopilot device has been provisioned without real evidence.","example":"Northstar's profile 'Learning - New Hire' should use user-driven enrollment and a pilot assignment. When selecting 'Create', inspect the saved record before testing any real device.","visual":["Select group","Set mode + naming","Review ESP","Save policy","Validate device setup"],"error":"Assigning every new corporate laptop to an untested deployment profile."}],"questions":[{"q":"Which Autopilot approach is most natural for an individually assigned new employee laptop?","a":["User-driven enrollment, after verifying prerequisites and assignment.","Self-deploying for every user without exception.","An Android dedicated-device profile.","Only a device compliance check without enrollment."],"why":"User-driven setups match a primary user sign-in; other modes have different intended use cases and prerequisites."},{"q":"A device stalls during the Enrollment Status Page required-app stage. What should you check?","a":["Required app assignments, detection rules, applicability and enrollment logs.","Only the computer's model name.","Only the Microsoft Entra display name of another user.","Whether the Windows desktop image looks attractive."],"why":"ESP depends on actual enrollment/app status, and detection or prerequisites can block progress."},{"q":"A saved Autopilot profile is displayed in the admin center. What does that prove?","a":["The profile object exists; it does not prove a physical device completed provisioning.","Every device in the tenant has completed setup.","All TPM attestations passed.","All Win32 app installation states are Succeeded."],"why":"Profile creation and real device enrollment are separate evidence steps."}],"artifact":{"collection":"autopilot","name":"Learning - New Hire","checks":{"mode":"User-driven","group":"Northstar - Pilot"},"proof":"A user-driven Windows Autopilot simulation profile assigned to Northstar - Pilot. A real OOBE test remains separate."},"docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/autopilot/overview"},{"id":"security","title":"Defender, encryption and safe endpoint security","domain":"Protection","lab":"S01","console":"security","objectiveIds":["SEC-049","SEC-051","SEC-046","SEC-048"],"difficulty":"Intermediate","time":"45–60 min","goal":"Differentiate Defender/EDR, antivirus, firewall, attack-surface reduction and BitLocker, then create and validate a pilot security policy.","chapters":[{"title":"Different controls protect different layers","teach":"Antivirus aims to detect and respond to malicious content. Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR) provides security telemetry and investigative capability. A firewall controls permitted network connections. Attack surface reduction limits behaviors that attackers commonly abuse. BitLocker protects data at rest if a device is lost or stolen. A security baseline bundles recommended settings but can overlap other policy settings. A device with working antivirus is not necessarily encrypted, and an encrypted device may still be compromised through unsafe applications or credentials.","example":"A lost laptop needs data-at-rest protection and recovery-key handling; a suspicious PowerShell process calls for endpoint investigation and potentially isolation, not merely disk encryption.","visual":["AV detection","EDR response","Firewall traffic","ASR behaviors","BitLocker at rest"],"error":"Assuming BitLocker encryption automatically prevents malware execution."},{"title":"Encrypt safely and manage recovery","teach":"A disk encryption deployment should be compatible with supported hardware, OS edition and chosen encryption method. Plan recovery-key backup and authorized retrieval before aggressively enforcing encryption. Rotate or recover keys through appropriate supported workflows, and limit who can view sensitive recovery information. Use an eligible pilot and observe encryption status separately from whether the policy record was created. On Windows, report categories can differ between configuration assignment and actual encryption progress; never infer 100% encryption from a successful policy assignment alone.","example":"Northstar deploys a disk encryption policy to its corporate Windows pilot, confirms key escrow in an authorized Microsoft environment, and checks the device's actual encryption state.","visual":["Prerequisites","Pilot policy","Recovery key escrow","Device encryption","Compliance status"],"error":"Losing access to a recovery key because recovery/escrow was not planned."},{"title":"Attack surface reduction needs testing","teach":"ASR rules and App Control policies can block unwanted behavior, but broad enablement can also disrupt legitimate line-of-business applications. Start with supported audit modes or narrow pilots where available, review events and exclusions, then enforce after verifying application impact. Avoid ignoring security recommendations: select evidence-based exceptions with an owner and expiration. Defender for Endpoint onboarding is a separate workflow from simply creating an antivirus policy; make sure a managed endpoint actually reports to the security service.","example":"A legacy payroll tool launches a child process that trips an ASR rule. Investigate the specific rule, event, process chain and business justification before allowing an exception.","visual":["Audit mode","Pilot observations","App impact","Enforcement","Monitor incidents"],"error":"Disabling all ASR rules after one application compatibility report."},{"title":"Use endpoint evidence to investigate","teach":"Security investigation should correlate Intune policy settings, device-side application, OS and encryption state, Defender sensor status and incidents. 'Policy Succeeded' does not prove the device is threat-free. An endpoint may have a stale Defender onboarding state, missing signature updates, an encryption status delay or a firewall policy conflict. In a production tenant, use the appropriate Defender and Intune logs, role-limited access and incident process before changing a security control. Record the observed before/after state and test rollback.","example":"Ticket: security team sees missing EDR telemetry. Confirm onboarding, connectivity, sensor health, Intune policy applicability, and Defender portal reporting separately.","visual":["Policy assignment","Device-side state","Defender telemetry","Incident investigation","Validated remediation"],"error":"Reporting the absence of Defender alerts as proof that EDR onboarding succeeded."}],"questions":[{"q":"A laptop is BitLocker encrypted but malware is detected. What does this demonstrate?","a":["Encryption at rest and malware prevention are separate security controls.","BitLocker automatically disables malware detection.","The compliance policy is necessarily broken.","The device cannot be Entra joined."],"why":"Defense in depth requires distinct controls for data-at-rest, process behavior, network connections and investigation."},{"q":"What must be planned before broad disk encryption enforcement?","a":["Supported endpoints, pilot behavior and secure recovery-key escrow/retrieval.","Only a new device naming convention.","Deleting all local recovery information first.","Disabling all policy status monitoring."],"why":"Encryption without recoverability can create unnecessary data-loss risk."},{"q":"A legitimate business app is blocked after ASR rollout. Which response is most responsible?","a":["Inspect the exact event/rule, verify a narrow exception or change in a pilot, and document risk.","Turn off all security controls company-wide.","Remove every security baseline without investigation.","Assume the block is unrelated to the rollout."],"why":"Investigate evidence and minimize the exception surface rather than globally weakening security."}],"artifact":{"collection":"security","name":"Learning - Encryption Enforce","checks":{"platform":"Windows","type":"Disk encryption","mode":"Enforce","group":"Northstar - Corporate Windows"},"sync":true,"proof":"A new enforce-mode Windows disk encryption policy assigned to Northstar - Corporate Windows and evaluated on an eligible simulated device."},"docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/mem/intune-service/protect/endpoint-security"},{"id":"apps","title":"Deploy a Win32 app and prove installation","domain":"Applications","lab":"A01","console":"apps","objectiveIds":["APP-061","APP-062","APP-069"],"difficulty":"Intermediate","time":"40–60 min","goal":"Understand packaging, detection, requirements, dependencies and assignment intent; prove a required app is actually installed on eligible endpoints.","chapters":[{"title":"Packaging is more than selecting an EXE","teach":"Win32 deployment requires a reproducible installer package, a supported silent installation command, any required uninstall command, proper install context, requirement rules, dependencies and detection logic. The Intune Management Extension is involved in Win32 deployment on supported Windows endpoints. An installer exiting with code 0 is not automatically proof that the intended application is present. A detection rule must report the actual installed artifact, such as a supported file, registry setting or product code. Test installer behavior on a disposable pilot before broad assignment.","example":"A tool installed for accountants requires a Visual C++ runtime and writes an MSI product code. Configure both requirements/dependencies and a reliable detection method.","visual":["Package app","Set install context","Define requirements","Choose detection","Assign + monitor"],"error":"Using a detection rule that always succeeds, producing false installation reporting."},{"title":"Assignment intent determines behavior","teach":"Required intent requests automatic installation on eligible targets; Available permits a user to initiate installation through supported user experiences; Uninstall requests removal where supported. Target groups, platform, enrollment, device/user context and assignment filters must be considered before deciding the intent. An Available assignment should not be interpreted as 'installed'. If an app has dependencies, its deployment order and status can affect the main app. Application supersedence is a different concept from simple dependency and should be tested independently.","example":"Deploy mandatory support software to a company-owned Windows pilot as Required; offer an optional design tool as Available to a specific user cohort.","visual":["Audience","Required vs Available","Eligibility","Dependency chain","Per-device result"],"error":"Calling an Available app assignment successful because it exists, despite the user never installing it."},{"title":"Troubleshoot the specific failure category","teach":"An app can be Not applicable because device requirements are not met, Pending because check-in hasn't occurred, Failed because of the installation command, or Installed but undetected due to poor detection logic. Examine per-device status, IME logs where applicable, the command line, exit codes, installation context, dependency state and the actual installed artifact. Repackaging the app without understanding the error may obscure the root cause. Use a reproducible test device and a stable detection rule; do not blindly switch to user context to fix a system-context app.","example":"An installer runs but Intune reports Failed. The app exists in Program Files, yet the detection rule checks a nonexistent registry path. Correct the rule and retest.","visual":["Received assignment","Ran installer","Exit code","Detection result","Status report"],"error":"Assuming application failure always means the package file itself is corrupt."},{"title":"Verify installation and protect the rollout","teach":"To close the support ticket, prove the intended app version and executable are present, the Intune device status reports the expected result, and a representative user can actually launch it. Monitor the pilot before expanding. For app protection, remember that company data restrictions and app install settings are separate policy categories. For Microsoft 365 Apps, servicing channels and cloud policy also require distinct verification; they are not a Win32 detection rule alone.","example":"Create 'Learning - Support Tools' as a simulated Required Windows Win32 deployment assigned to Northstar - Corporate Windows, sync a matching device and inspect the simulated installed-app list.","visual":["Required assignment","Eligible device sync","Install + detect","Verify app use","Expand pilot"],"error":"Reporting that all employees have a required app based on a saved assignment but zero device reports."}],"questions":[{"q":"A Win32 installer exits successfully but Intune reports the app not detected. What should you inspect?","a":["Detection rules and actual installed artifacts on the target device.","Only the tenant's color theme.","Only a Windows Autopilot name template.","Whether a different user's iPhone is compliant."],"why":"Intune app installation status relies on a successful install and compatible detection evidence."},{"q":"An application is assigned as Available. What does that mean?","a":["Eligible users can choose to install it through a supported experience; it is not automatically installed.","Intune forces installation on every device at the next sync.","The app will always bypass requirement rules.","Every dependency is automatically removed."],"why":"Available is an optional assignment intent, distinct from Required or Uninstall."},{"q":"A Win32 app only works with a specified runtime. Which deployment feature is relevant?","a":["Requirements or dependency configuration, based on the runtime and packaging design.","Only a Conditional Access grant control.","Only a BitLocker key rotation.","Only an enrollment restriction for Android."],"why":"App prerequisites must be evaluated before or during the installation workflow."}],"artifact":{"collection":"apps","name":"Learning - Support Tools","checks":{"platform":"Windows","type":"Win32","intent":"Required","group":"Northstar - Corporate Windows"},"sync":true,"proof":"A new Required Windows Win32 app assigned to Northstar - Corporate Windows, with a matching simulated device reporting it installed after sync."},"docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/mem/intune-service/apps/apps-win32-app-management"},{"id":"updates","title":"Windows update rings versus feature updates","domain":"Protection","lab":"S02","console":"updates","objectiveIds":["SEC-054","SEC-055","SEC-056","SEC-060"],"difficulty":"Intermediate","time":"35–50 min","goal":"Differentiate deferrals, deadlines, Windows feature updates, quality updates, Autopatch, and device-reported servicing state.","chapters":[{"title":"Quality updates and feature updates are not interchangeable","teach":"Windows quality updates deliver servicing and security fixes for an existing Windows release. Feature updates move Windows to a newer feature release. Update rings help configure user experience and servicing timing such as deferrals, deadlines and restart behavior. Separately managed feature/quality update policies or Windows Autopatch may contribute to targeting and orchestration. Each mechanism has supported prerequisites and precedence considerations. Avoid assuming that one ring name fully describes the desired Windows release or that a deadline guarantees a successful installation on an offline device.","example":"A security patch must arrive promptly, while a new Windows feature release should roll out gradually after compatibility testing.","visual":["Quality update servicing","Feature release targeting","Update ring timing","Restart UX","Compliance + reporting"],"error":"Changing the feature update target when the real need is an urgent monthly quality update."},{"title":"Deferrals and deadlines solve different problems","teach":"A deferral changes when an update is offered to eligible devices. A deadline helps control how much time can pass before installation or restart enforcement, subject to the applicable servicing policy and restart behavior. A shorter deadline can improve security posture but also create operational disruption. Choose pilot/cohort assignments that reflect business hours, device role and risk. Windows Autopatch manages update deployment groups and processes under qualifying conditions; it is not equivalent to creating one ordinary update ring.","example":"IT pilots quality updates quickly; finance staff receives validated updates with an appropriately planned restart deadline before payroll day.","visual":["Offer after deferral","Install attempt","Pending restart","Deadline","Reported update state"],"error":"Using a one-day restart deadline for all devices without considering critical shared workstations."},{"title":"Update reports are evidence, not scheduling promises","teach":"A device may be assigned a ring yet remain unpatched because it is offline, incompatible, blocked by safeguard holds, low on disk space, or awaiting a restart. Investigate the actual update and OS build, last scan, telemetry freshness, applicable policies, error codes and relevant client-side logs. For macOS, iOS and Android, update control methods differ, so do not copy Windows settings blindly. A 'Succeeded' assignment may only prove policy reception, not the installation of a given cumulative update.","example":"The update ring exists and is assigned, but 12 laptops show a stale OS build. Compare their last check-in and required reboot state before modifying deferrals.","visual":["Assigned policy","Device scan","Download/install","Restart","Build verification"],"error":"Calling the rollout complete because the ring itself was saved successfully."},{"title":"Run a staged, reversible pilot","teach":"Build a small Windows update pilot group, configure a ring with moderate deferrals and deadlines, inspect eligibility and the intended user experience, and verify actual device servicing after deployment. Maintain a rollback plan for problems such as problematic drivers, blocked apps and incompatible devices. Hotpatch applicability depends on a supported edition and service prerequisites; it cannot be enabled universally. Document which servicing technology is authoritative for each cohort to avoid unintended conflicts.","example":"Create the simulated 'Learning - Quality Ring' targeted to Northstar - Pilot with a modest quality deferral. The simulator validates the saved ring; only a real device can prove the patch installed.","visual":["Pilot ring","Review targeting","Wait for scan","Inspect errors","Expand or pause"],"error":"Deferring every security update to resolve one unrelated app issue."}],"questions":[{"q":"A laptop has the correct update ring but remains on an old OS build. What should you do?","a":["Inspect update scan/install status, connectivity, restart, compatibility and device logs.","Assume the ring name proves the OS updated.","Immediately wipe the laptop.","Remove all Microsoft Entra device objects."],"why":"An assigned servicing configuration does not prove a physical update installed."},{"q":"What is the primary role of a quality update deferral?","a":["Delay when eligible quality updates are offered according to supported policy settings.","Choose the user principal name.","Install Microsoft 365 Apps automatically.","Force disk encryption key escrow."],"why":"Deferral controls offering timing, separate from the update installation result."},{"q":"A new Windows release is planned for 200 users. Which strategy reduces impact?","a":["Pilot compatibility and expand through monitored cohorts with a rollback/escalation plan.","Force every device immediately without telemetry.","Disable security monitoring throughout rollout.","Assume Windows 365 and physical Windows updates are identical."],"why":"A measured rollout enables evidence-driven change management."}],"artifact":{"collection":"updates","name":"Learning - Quality Ring","checks":{"group":"Northstar - Pilot"},"proof":"A saved pilot Windows update ring named Learning - Quality Ring, targeted to Northstar - Pilot. Device update installation is not verified by this simulator."},"docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/mem/intune-service/protect/windows-update-for-business-configure"},{"id":"automation","title":"Safe PowerShell, Graph and remediation workflows","domain":"Operations","lab":"O01","console":"scripts","objectiveIds":["OPE-073","OPE-077","OPE-080"],"difficulty":"Advanced","time":"40–60 min","goal":"Explain read-only discovery, authorization scopes, pagination, retry handling, idempotent remediation and safe deployment of PowerShell scripts.","chapters":[{"title":"Start with the least privileged operation","teach":"Microsoft Graph makes tenant data and management operations accessible through an API. An automation needs the correct identity type, permissions and administrator consent where required. Reading managed devices and changing a policy are different authority levels. Before writing a change script, use safe read-only discovery to understand the target schema and returned data. Never include a real secret in the training simulator or commit tokens to a repository. Delegated and application permissions have different security contexts; prefer the smallest privilege and authenticate appropriately.","example":"You need a monthly inventory export. Use an authorized read-only device-management scope rather than granting broad policy-write permissions just because the same API offers them.","visual":["Identity + consent","Request scope","Read inventory","Validate response","Log results"],"error":"Using Global Administrator credentials for a basic read-only inventory report."},{"title":"Handle pagination and service throttling","teach":"Microsoft Graph list endpoints may paginate results; a client must follow documented next links to collect the complete inventory rather than assuming the first page contains every device. Graph services can throttle requests, often returning HTTP 429 and a Retry-After value. Respect server guidance, use bounded exponential backoff where appropriate, and avoid sending parallel bursts that create more failures. Distinguish an authorization failure from a missing record and a transient service response. Store only necessary data and follow retention requirements for generated reports.","example":"A script returns 100 of 217 devices. Investigate pagination and the nextLink before concluding the tenant is missing 117 endpoints.","visual":["GET devices","Receive page","Follow nextLink","Handle 429","Complete export"],"error":"Assuming Graph returned every object because the HTTP request was 200 OK."},{"title":"Detection and remediation are two different stages","teach":"Intune Remediations commonly use a detection script to determine whether a problem exists and a remediation script to attempt a safe correction, subject to supported prerequisites and licensing. Good remediation is idempotent, scoped, observable, tested on a pilot, and reversible where practical. It should not restart every device or delete business data merely because one service is unhealthy. Check execution context (System versus User), scripts' exit/status behavior, detection results and logs. A script stored in the Academy is sample text only; the offline platform must never execute it.","example":"A print service may be stopped unexpectedly. Detect the real service state, restart only when appropriate, and verify that the subsequent detection passes.","visual":["Detection","Problem found?","Safe remediation","Verification","Report + rollback"],"error":"A remediation reports success while the detection script still finds the service stopped."},{"title":"Use telemetry to prove improved operations","teach":"Automation success requires observing the result in the intended population, not merely receiving a job accepted message. For scripts, capture execution report, output/error information, affected devices and detection after remediation. For Graph, validate data count, access scope and handling of failures. For Security Copilot or agent recommendations, humans must review scope, effect and permissions before accepting potentially disruptive changes. Treat recommendations as inputs to an operational decision, not authorization to act on every endpoint automatically.","example":"Northstar can prove a maintenance script fixed 15 of 17 affected pilot devices, can name the two failures, and has an escalation plan for them.","visual":["Define metric","Pilot job","Collect results","Investigate exceptions","Document decision"],"error":"Reporting '100% remediated' because all commands were submitted, despite no validation of the device state."}],"questions":[{"q":"Graph returns the first page of managed devices and a nextLink. What should the client do?","a":["Follow documented pagination until the required collection is complete.","Assume the first page is the entire fleet.","Delete older records to reduce the page count.","Turn off auditing."],"why":"Graph collections may paginate; a single successful request does not guarantee completeness."},{"q":"A Graph API call receives HTTP 429. Which behavior is appropriate?","a":["Respect Retry-After and implement bounded backoff/retry behavior.","Immediately send hundreds more requests in parallel.","Assume the current account has been deleted.","Wipe local Windows configuration."],"why":"Throttling requires respecting service rate guidance."},{"q":"A remediation script is safe to schedule broadly only after what evidence?","a":["Pilot results, idempotence/scope review, detection verification and a rollback/escalation plan.","The script is over 100 lines long.","One user says it looks fine.","The author is a Global Administrator."],"why":"Scripts should be narrowly scoped, measured and repeatable without unintended side effects."}],"artifact":{"collection":"scripts","name":"Learning - Service Health","checks":{"type":"Remediation","runAs":"System","group":"Northstar - IT Pilot"},"proof":"A saved simulated remediation configuration named Learning - Service Health; execution still requires an authorized Microsoft tenant."},"docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/use-the-api"},{"id":"analytics","title":"Read the signals before making a change","domain":"Operations","lab":"O03","console":"reports","objectiveIds":["OPE-078","OPE-079","OPE-081","OPE-082","OPE-083"],"difficulty":"Intermediate","time":"30–45 min","goal":"Differentiate inventory dashboards, Endpoint Analytics, device health, incident evidence and AI-generated recommendations.","chapters":[{"title":"Counts are not explanations","teach":"An Intune dashboard can summarize how many devices are enrolled, compliant, or failing an app installation. An aggregate rate helps identify trends but not necessarily why a particular device failed. Device-specific evidence includes OS/build, last check-in, policy assignment, reporting freshness and failure messages. A percentage without population size can mislead. Device and user privacy also matters: collect only the information needed to solve the problem and limit who can access sensitive reports.","example":"The fleet is 94% compliant, yet the six noncompliant finance laptops share a missing encryption requirement. Inspect the cohort and per-setting results rather than congratulating the whole fleet.","visual":["Fleet trend","Scope affected group","Drill into device","Trace policy","Validate fix"],"error":"Declaring a repair successful because an overall score improved while the affected team still fails."},{"title":"Endpoint Analytics measures experience, not just security","teach":"Endpoint Analytics and supported advanced capabilities provide signals such as startup performance and app reliability. A secure device can still have poor user experience due to repeated restarts, app crashes or slow startup. Use baselines and compare devices in a meaningful cohort; different device classes and network conditions can change expected performance. Remediations may address a specific detectable issue, but they should have measurable success criteria and not simply run blindly because a score is low.","example":"Employees complain that login takes several minutes. Check startup performance and relevant device/app telemetry rather than applying a firewall exception by guesswork.","visual":["User complaint","Collect startup signal","Find driver/process","Target pilot","Compare before/after"],"error":"Interpreting a high security score as proof that application startup is fast."},{"title":"Use logs and agent recommendations responsibly","teach":"Security Copilot agents and related assisted analysis can suggest investigations or management actions based on available signals. They do not remove the need for administrator judgment, appropriate licenses or safe authorization. Confirm the underlying data is current, examine any proposed blast radius, check whether a recommendation conflicts with business requirements and request the right approvals before changing policy. Microsoft Defender investigations and Intune policy reports may reflect different aspects of the same incident and should be correlated carefully.","example":"An assistant suggests disabling a broad security rule after one false positive. Validate the precise rule, affected app and targeted exception instead of automatically accepting a tenant-wide change.","visual":["Gather telemetry","Agent suggestion","Human review","Pilot change","Document outcome"],"error":"Executing a potentially destructive recommendation without reviewing scope and evidence."},{"title":"Build an operational baseline and alert path","teach":"Track service health, message center communications, enrollment failure rate, compliance drift, application failures, update freshness and user-experience metrics. Alerts should route to a responsible team with clear severity and action instructions. Distinguish new incidents from reporting delays so operators do not create repeated unnecessary changes. Exported reports must follow access and retention requirements. A useful operational baseline makes it possible to say whether a change genuinely improved reliability after rollout.","example":"A weekly report compares device enrollment errors and app installation failures against the previous period, then directs recurring issues to the appropriate support queue.","visual":["Define baseline","Monitor signals","Detect drift","Investigate and act","Measure improvement"],"error":"Configuring alerts without owners or thresholds and overwhelming the service desk."}],"questions":[{"q":"A fleet compliance rate increased after a change. What extra evidence establishes that the original problem was fixed?","a":["Per-device results for the affected cohort and confirmation of the specific failed setting.","Only the dashboard's background color.","Only the number of Entra tenant administrators.","Only the number of cloud applications licensed."],"why":"Aggregate improvements do not prove the specific affected population recovered."},{"q":"What does Endpoint Analytics help investigate?","a":["User experience signals such as startup performance and app reliability.","Only raw network packet captures.","Only domain-registration billing.","Only iCloud personal photo storage."],"why":"Endpoint Analytics evaluates supported endpoint experience and reliability data."},{"q":"A Security Copilot agent suggests changing a policy for every device. What should an administrator do first?","a":["Review evidence, blast radius, required approvals and a narrowly targeted pilot.","Approve automatically because it came from AI.","Disable security logging to avoid alerts.","Remove the tenant's emergency accounts."],"why":"Human validation and least-privilege change management remain mandatory."}],"artifact":null,"docs":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/mem/intune-service/fundamentals/reports"}];
